INT. STILT CLINIC - EXAM ROOM - NIGHT

A rural clinic built on pilings over a flooded delta. One lamp, one cot, one oxygen cylinder. Rain hammers the tin roof. Through the slats, brown water slides past.

NURSE ADWOA FOSU-LANGLEY, 30s, kneels by the cot, a stethoscope hooked in her ears. On the cot lies an unconscious child, eight, breathing in shallow sips. Beside the cot, a satellite phone on speaker, its antenna angled out the window.

ADWOA
(into the phone)
Doctor, he's at eighty-two percent. His lips are blue.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
How long since the last dose?

ADWOA
Forty minutes.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
Give him another.

ADWOA
It's the last vial.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
Then it's the last vial.

Adwoa loads a syringe with trembling hands. She glances at the child, then at the phone, then back at the syringe. She finds a vein on the small wrist.

ADWOA
Doctor Kwan, she's not breathing deep enough.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
Who isn't? You or the boy?

ADWOA
The boy. And I've got no one to hold him.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
Then you hold him. She can't see you through a phone, so tell me what you see.

Adwoa takes the child's hand in both of hers and kneels on the wet planks. She pushes the plunger.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
Watch his chest.

ADWOA
It's rising.

DR. KWAN (V.O.)
(over phone)
Eighty-five?

ADWOA
Eighty-seven.

Adwoa sits back and rests her forehead against the cot. Rain drums on.
